WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Thank you.

00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:11.000
All right, well, welcome everybody. This is our course on Principles of information quality

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:19.000
It's generally a gateway to our program, and so for that reason, what I like to do

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:24.000
is talk about the overall information quality program in general

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:36.000
And after that, I'll talk about the policies for this particular course. And if we have time, I may get started with some of the content. But mainly today I wanted to kind of give an overview

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:44.000
So of the graduate program, some of you are in the master's, some of you are in the PhD

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Anyway, I think this might be helpful for you

00:00:50.000 --> 00:01:03.000
So first of all, want to start off that we all kind of agree, or well, you don't have to agree, but this is the way we define information quality. And of course there's a lot of ways to do it

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:11.000
Most people, when you talk about quality, they just say accuracy. Another common one is fitness for use

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Others say, well, it's accurate complete and they start giving a lot of characteristics

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:21.000
Right information, right time, right place

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:28.000
more of a practice type of definition

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:38.000
And really, underneath is this one is pretty accurate. It's the application of total quality management principles to Building Information Products

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:44.000
It's not the formal definition, but this is where information quality as a discipline came from

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:48.000
So back in the 1980s

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:54.000
There was a movement in manufacturing called total quality management

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.000
And it really started in Japan

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:12.000
As you may… well, I'm sure you don't remember, but you may know that after World War II, after the United States destroyed all of Japan's manufacturing, they got a chance to start all over again

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
And we help them. And part of that help was that some of the people here in the U.S.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
who were advocates of quality went over there to help the Japanese

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:33.000
Deming was one of them, a very important one. And Deming had many principles about

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:37.000
how you create a quality product

00:02:37.000 --> 00:02:39.000
And

00:02:39.000 --> 00:02:53.000
Anyway, the Japanese were very excited about it and they adopted it and as you may know also, they adopted it so well that they almost took over the car market. They actually

00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:03.000
take away a huge chunk of the U.S. car market because they manufactured automobiles even today, like Toyotas and Hondas

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:07.000
that were super high quality, reliable, dependable

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:14.000
And so then it came back to the US

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:17.000
And of course, now just about everybody

00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:22.000
in manufacturing follows total quality management principles. As a matter of fact

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:27.000
The International Organization for Standardization called ISO

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:34.000
has enshrined the total quality management in some

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:46.000
standards, the ISO 9000 standards, and we'll talk more about that later. But anyway, so what happened is that

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:51.000
Before I get into our definition, finish this thought here

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:57.000
is that early on in the later 1980s and early 1990s

00:03:57.000 --> 00:04:02.000
The people who were in the data management industry

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:06.000
And especially in the academic side at MIT

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:20.000
They saw this, and they said, you know, the way to think about data and how we're using data is that we're really manufacturing something. We're manufacturing data or information product

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.000
We're taking raw data, processing it through, and we're not using

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:37.000
mechanical machines, but we're using computers. We're using these computers to manipulate the raw data into an information product

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:54.000
We take data and make it into information by adding context around it, bringing things together. And so by taking that idea that in data management, we're really manufacturing information products

00:04:54.000 --> 00:05:05.000
And that we should manage information as a product, then why reinvent the wheel? Let's take the principles that have already been developed in manufacturing and apply them

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:07.000
to data

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.000
And so this is we have the discipline of

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Information quality has

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:19.000
has evolved, and it's the reason then that I like to make

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:25.000
A distinction between information quality and data quality. Now, not everybody does

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:29.000
But since I'm the teacher, then I can do whatever I want to

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.000
So

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:36.000
So information qualities and information science discipline

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:47.000
And of course, hopefully by now you understand the difference between information science and computer science. Again, it's the same kind of two sides of the coin

00:05:47.000 --> 00:06:04.000
Computer science is about the computers and the hardware and the software, and the information science is about the data and the information. And of course they work together. So as a matter of fact, I don't know if you're aware or not, but there has been a change in alignment here

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:13.000
at the university, and we no longer have a Department of Computer Science and a Department of Information Science.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:16.000
As of this summer

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:18.000
As of July the 1st

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:35.000
These two departments were merged together into a school, so we now have the school of computer and information sciences and of course this program is a part of that school. So we have a new school here on campus

00:06:35.000 --> 00:06:38.000
The School of Computer and Information Sciences

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:44.000
So, information quality is one of the information sciences, so it's a discipline

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:55.000
And it's concerned with two main things. The first one is maximizing the value and minimizing the risk of an organization's information assets

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:59.000
So again, in this idea of thinking of

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:17.000
of information quality or information in a manufacturing sense right information is an asset. It's what we use to create value for the organization. Just think about here at the University

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:24.000
One of the information products that we manufacture is your transcript

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:34.000
So the transcript has many feeds into it from different classes that you take. The grades that you receive, and so forth

00:07:34.000 --> 00:07:50.000
These were brought together, integrated together, and put together, and the product is this transcript. It's very important. It helps you keep track of where you are, but more importantly, once you've finished all the requirements, which again, they go from your transcript

00:07:50.000 --> 00:08:06.000
then it becomes a document, often now a digital document that you, for example, you did the master's and you have your transcript showing you are awarded the master's degree. This is typically what you give

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:23.000
As proof to an employer that you've completed your master's degree. Or if you go on to, say, a PhD degree at another school, again, they're going to request and say, we need proof that you've already achieved your master's. And so the transcript would be the way that that

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:33.000
is communicated. So that's an information product. Similarly, in a hospital, the same sort of thing happens in your medical chart

00:08:33.000 --> 00:08:50.000
You may have many encounters in this hospital, other hospitals, outpatient, inpatient. All these are gathered together, and it's very important that all these are collected accurately and that your chart reflects everything that's happened to you

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.000
And not anything that didn't happen to you from some other patient

00:08:54.000 --> 00:08:59.000
So creating these information products

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:00.000
Now

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:05.000
So the organization that's creating here at the university

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:12.000
We create your transcript. That's our information product that we create. You're the consumer of that

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:13.000
So

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:30.000
We want to maximize the value of our information at the university. And so this is one way we do it. We also do it by taking the enrollment data and analyzing it to see if we're doing a good job, if we can recruit students, how the students are doing

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:46.000
What's causing students to fall out of the classes and leave the university? So we do a lot of analysis of this data, and we create a lot of those information products out of that data. All kinds of analyses and reports

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:51.000
about student continuation through the program.

00:09:51.000 --> 00:10:02.000
So that's important, and it creates value for the organization. It helps us maintain enrollments or increase enrollments, prevent students from getting at risk

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:10.000
And so that's from the internal side, the people who are producing or the organization that's producing these products

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:12.000
But the other side of the coin

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:19.000
is that we want to produce information products that create value for the users of those products.

00:10:19.000 --> 00:10:24.000
And so, again, looking at the transcript idea

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:40.000
We want it to be valuable to you, and it is valuable to you. It's your proof that you've completed all your requirements, you've received this degree from an accredited university, and of course, there are many other products that we produce for other consumers. For example

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:46.000
University is required to report to the Arkansas Department of Education

00:10:46.000 --> 00:11:02.000
And they want to know, you know, who are the students, how are they doing? What majors do they have? What's their progress? How many of them are completing their degrees, how many are falling out and not completing their degrees

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:17.000
And they used this information and it's valuable to them. But again, at the same time it becomes valuable to the university because it's those reports that we get reimbursement or get funding from the state

00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:20.000
for our students that we

00:11:20.000 --> 00:11:22.000
Enroll

00:11:22.000 --> 00:11:34.000
So again, there's two sides of the coin in the use of information. The value to the owner or the organization that has that information that's creating these information products

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:40.000
And the other side is we want it to create value for the consumers of those products.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:46.000
So anyway, the key word in our definition is value

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:50.000
It's all about creating value from information.

00:11:50.000 --> 00:12:04.000
Now, we'll talk a little bit more about the difference between information quality and data quality as we go on. But this is our definition. So we call our program an information quality program

00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:14.000
It's an information quality master of science information quality track in the PhD program and information quality

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:16.000
a graduate certificate

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:19.000
And those are the three degrees that we offer

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:27.000
Now, not long ago, an organization did a certification of information quality

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:33.000
It was IQCP, Information Quality Certified Professional

00:12:33.000 --> 00:12:37.000
And in order to do that, they had to go through quite a bit of a study

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:45.000
And part of the study resulted in what's called the Information Quality Knowledge and Practice

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:52.000
And so they categorized the knowledge and practice into

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.000
six groups

00:12:54.000 --> 00:13:05.000
And the first one, again, coincides with our definition, information quality value and business impact, or should say organizational impact

00:13:05.000 --> 00:13:21.000
It's not just businesses that use information, it's a lot of nonprofits like hospitals and governmental organizations. Obviously, the federal government, state government, they use a lot of information and create a lot of information products

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:26.000
And the question is, what's the value and impact of that?

00:13:26.000 --> 00:13:33.000
And then another that's become extremely important in recent days or recent years

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:37.000
is this idea of information quality, strategy, and governance

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:48.000
Back in the day, we just thought of information as a commodity data, something we would get and use. It was all about creating the programs

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:50.000
Back in the day when

00:13:50.000 --> 00:14:01.000
I don't know, I forget the exact number, but it was like 70% of freshmen entering MIT were going into computer science and programming because it was all about

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:09.000
creating these clever processes that would create these products, but the emphasis was just on the data

00:14:09.000 --> 00:14:17.000
I'm sorry, just on the processing, the computer programming part of it, and not really about the data itself. But now

00:14:17.000 --> 00:14:24.000
That's not such a big deal because as you're probably aware and may be doing right now

00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:32.000
Computer programming is kind of falling by the wayside. Software engineering is being taken over by AI

00:14:32.000 --> 00:14:37.000
So, it's pretty easy now to use Claude or some of these other

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:42.000
language models to create all kinds of programs for you.

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:51.000
And so the need for having a staff of computer programmers, very knowledgeable about different types of languages and so forth

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.000
is not as distinctive, it's not

00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:05.000
Back when you had companies and they could afford to hire a lot of programmers, they had a huge advantage over small companies. But that's really not true anymore.

00:15:05.000 --> 00:15:17.000
So what is the competitive advantage that these companies have? It's not the programming anymore. It's not having data centers anymore or

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:28.000
maintaining their own equipment. They don't have to have raised floors and have people that take care of their computers for them because now we have cloud computing

00:15:28.000 --> 00:15:32.000
So the thing that's left that helps

00:15:32.000 --> 00:15:38.000
distinguish one organization from another, especially if they're in the same kind of business

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:41.000
is the data that they have

00:15:41.000 --> 00:15:54.000
And so, you need strategies for what data do we need to acquire, what kind of information products do we need to build to be competitive, and how do we maintain control over that data

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:56.000
And that's the idea of data governance

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:08.000
So that's become very important in the last few years, this idea of strategy. Now most matter of fact, all governmental agencies are required

00:16:08.000 --> 00:16:12.000
to have what's called a chief data officer

00:16:12.000 --> 00:16:19.000
And one of the most important roles of a chief data officer is to create a data strategy for the company.

00:16:19.000 --> 00:16:26.000
So strategy and then governance have become very important. Governance again because

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:42.000
You want to maintain control of data, but then there are also a lot of regulations around data. For example, here at the university, we're governed by a law called FERPA, which has to do with educational data, so it governs

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:52.000
What data that we can release, what data is considered protected. And so we have to be very careful about that and meet all of those

00:16:52.000 --> 00:16:56.000
requirements. So that's part of governance.

00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:04.000
And then information quality, environment, and culture. This is very important because

00:17:04.000 --> 00:17:11.000
the culture of an organization determines how it does business, how it gets things done

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:21.000
And this has really become extremely important in the last few years with the emergence of AI. We've had many things over the years that have changed

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:36.000
culture from big data now into AI and we need to think about things in a different way. So the idea of change in culture, matter of fact, one of the courses that we teach here

00:17:36.000 --> 00:17:39.000
this project and change management

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:53.000
And in change management, we don't mean, like, change and releases of code. We're talking about change in culture. How do you change culture so that you keep up? And we look at some use cases

00:17:53.000 --> 00:18:01.000
Now, use cases, but we'll look at some examples of what's happened to those people that are not willing to change their culture

00:18:01.000 --> 00:18:03.000
For example, Kodak

00:18:03.000 --> 00:18:11.000
that dominated, literally dominated the world in photography is now just a small company

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:16.000
And the reason that they lost their lead was that they just didn't

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:26.000
change their ideas about how photography was going to be done. They always thought it was going to be done with film, that digital was just a fad

00:18:26.000 --> 00:18:29.000
And so they failed to change and keep up

00:18:29.000 --> 00:18:39.000
and create the environment necessary to do that. So you'll notice that when talking about information quality knowledge and practice, and these six main groups

00:18:39.000 --> 00:18:45.000
of knowledge and practice, that the first three of them here

00:18:45.000 --> 00:18:57.000
really don't have anything to do about dirty data. It's not talking about whether data is timely, whether it's accurate, it's talking about the management of information

00:18:57.000 --> 00:19:01.000
So, that's the reason that our program here

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:07.000
Israeli could be over in the business school as well as here in… but we ended up in the

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:09.000
College of

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:13.000
Science, technology, engineering, and mathematics

00:19:13.000 --> 00:19:20.000
Which, again, so we're sort of half and half, and many people that join our program

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:25.000
do it because of that, because we have a strong management component to our program

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:31.000
And it's not all just a technical program, but we do get into the technical part

00:19:31.000 --> 00:19:39.000
So we'll talk about information quality measurement and improvement, right? You can't manage something that you can't measure

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:47.000
So how do you measure the quality of information and data? And how do you improve it

00:19:47.000 --> 00:20:00.000
And how do you sustain that palsy over time? You need programs to do that. It's not just something you do as a one-time project. You need to constantly be aware of it

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:05.000
And then another thing that many people don't think about is

00:20:05.000 --> 00:20:15.000
The quality of your information and your information products often depends on the architecture that you've built. Your information architecture

00:20:15.000 --> 00:20:29.000
And sometimes it's just not adequate to maintain the quality or perform the way that you want it to. So you do have to pay attention to information architectures, and these are constantly changing, right? So

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:34.000
We go from client-server to

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:42.000
Big data, and now we're in an AI, we're talking about Agentic architectures. So there are many things that happen

00:20:42.000 --> 00:20:44.000
Now, as I mentioned a while ago

00:20:44.000 --> 00:20:54.000
This is the Internet IQ International that developed this, the six domains. They did quite a bit of work in doing it

00:20:54.000 --> 00:21:02.000
This part is what we call data quality, as opposed to the overall idea of information quality

00:21:02.000 --> 00:21:04.000
So data quality

00:21:04.000 --> 00:21:10.000
is where we make requirements for the data

00:21:10.000 --> 00:21:24.000
And so quality is meeting requirements. This is the ISO definition of quality. Quality is meeting requirements. So data quality is data meeting data quality requirements.

00:21:24.000 --> 00:21:29.000
And so that's an important part of information quality

00:21:29.000 --> 00:21:45.000
But it's not all of it, so that's the reason that we make a distinction between data quality and information quality. This is an information quality program that includes these top three things, this idea of value, strategy, governance, environment, and culture.

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:53.000
These are all important parts. It's more than just measuring the amount of missing data that you have in a database.

00:21:53.000 --> 00:22:06.000
That's a data quality issue. That's a data quality measurement. I can set up a metric and I can measure how many of the names and addresses in my database, my customer database, have missing zip codes

00:22:06.000 --> 00:22:18.000
That's a very good and valid data quality requirement. I can say I'm requiring, you know, I can't really do business

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:29.000
If I don't have that to be 90%, I need 90% of my customer mailing addresses to have an accurate zip code. So how many of them are missing a zip code?

00:22:29.000 --> 00:22:39.000
That doesn't talk about the accuracy, that would be another measurement. How many of the zip codes that I have for my customer names and addresses are actually accurate

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:53.000
It's a different question of whether they're there or not. So there are many, many types of metrics and measurements that you can set up and improve them, right? So, if my goal is 90% accuracy, or 90% completeness

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:55.000
No zip codes, and I'm only at 80%

00:22:55.000 --> 00:23:07.000
Maybe I launch a project, I need to go out and increase the number of zip code that I have so that I can meet that requirement

00:23:07.000 --> 00:23:15.000
Okay, feel free if you have any questions as we go along to speak up

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:24.000
So hopefully that gives you a better understanding of what information quality is all about. It's not just about cleaning up dirty data

00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:28.000
It's also about those other things, the strategy and the value creation

00:23:28.000 --> 00:23:35.000
So why study it? Well, it's important because poor data quality or information quality

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:39.000
And again, either one you can look at in this sense

00:23:39.000 --> 00:23:49.000
cost companies billions of dollars each year. So much of the time that employees spend is getting data ready to use

00:23:49.000 --> 00:24:01.000
And mistakes with it, especially in the medical side, or even the military side, there are very famous example during the war in Kosovo several years ago

00:24:01.000 --> 00:24:06.000
They wanted to bomb a headquarters of the enemy

00:24:06.000 --> 00:24:15.000
But it turned out that the headquarters had moved, but the information, the geographical information had not been updated, and they ended up

00:24:15.000 --> 00:24:25.000
bombing a hospital instead of military headquarters. So a lot of innocent lives were lost

00:24:25.000 --> 00:24:33.000
And of course, as I mentioned, this rise of AI, especially generative AI, has given a new focus because

00:24:33.000 --> 00:24:41.000
Many organizations are realizing that their AI models are only as good as the data that they use to train them

00:24:41.000 --> 00:24:50.000
And then on the reverse of that, Agentic AI is a way to use AI to improve the quality of data

00:24:50.000 --> 00:24:57.000
So Agentic data quality is a new discipline that we'll talk about and actually

00:24:57.000 --> 00:25:04.000
We'll do some labs around that so that you can build some agents to improve data quality

00:25:04.000 --> 00:25:11.000
So you can make a difference in studying information quality through our program

00:25:11.000 --> 00:25:19.000
And I like to think that we're a community more than just students support each other

00:25:19.000 --> 00:25:30.000
respect each other. I know now that so many people are online, but still, we need to remain connected. One of the things I'm going to urge you to do as we get a little farther into this

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:34.000
is joined. We have a LinkedIn group for our program

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:46.000
And there are many of our graduates are in there, many of the people who employ our graduates are part of that network. So you need to think about building your network

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:51.000
Building your professional credentials, what you're doing by going through these graduate programs

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:58.000
And gaining a professional experience. And so here's the LinkedIn group. So I urge you all to join this

00:25:58.000 --> 00:26:03.000
It's called the UALR Information Quality Graduate Program

00:26:03.000 --> 00:26:07.000
And many of our, most of our graduates

00:26:07.000 --> 00:26:13.000
professionals are… and a lot of our students are in that group

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:21.000
So again, this is a little bit dated right here. I need to update the slide. The Information Science Department

00:26:21.000 --> 00:26:28.000
Yeah, definitely, because Dr. Pierce just retired this year, so I need to update my slides

00:26:28.000 --> 00:26:41.000
And again, what's happened is that now since her retirement, it triggered this merger of the information science department and the computer science into this new school

00:26:41.000 --> 00:26:46.000
But we still have these programs. It hasn't changed the programs that we offer

00:26:46.000 --> 00:26:48.000
So we offer three programs

00:26:48.000 --> 00:27:05.000
A graduate certificate in information quality, a Master of Science in Information Quality, and a PhD in Computer and Information Sciences with an emphasis or with a track in information quality.

00:27:05.000 --> 00:27:19.000
