And its headquarters now is over in California

00:30:42.000 --> 00:30:48.000
But at the time, Axiom was very important Arkansas company

00:30:48.000 --> 00:30:59.000
And they had a lot of vision. Charles Morgan was an Arkansan himself, and wanted to promote technology and progress in Arkansas. So he helped fund it

00:30:59.000 --> 00:31:04.000
Also, as I mentioned, MIT was one of the early

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:18.000
universities to see information quality as a discipline. And they're the ones who really developed this idea of managing information as a product and applying these

00:31:18.000 --> 00:31:24.000
Total quality management principles to managing information quality

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:38.000
As a matter of fact, they developed what they called the total data quality management, TDQM instead of TQM, as a way of enshrining this idea of applying manufacturing principles

00:31:38.000 --> 00:31:42.000
producing information products

00:31:42.000 --> 00:31:48.000
And then at the time, this was not the college, it was not the STEM college

00:31:48.000 --> 00:31:53.000
It was called the College of Engineering and Information Technology

00:31:53.000 --> 00:31:59.000
And the founding dean of that college, Dr. Mary Good, who has since passed away

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:12.000
was willing to house the program. So we had the three main ingredients. We had funding from Axiom, we had intellectual property that had already been developed at MIT

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:16.000
And then we had a university that was willing to house the program

00:32:16.000 --> 00:32:23.000
the Donaghy College. And so we put all those together and got started in 2006

